<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Formula map</p>
  <h2>What each listed ingredient contributes</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Glucose</h3><p>Provides carbohydrate and participates in intestinal sodium-glucose cotransport. The transport mechanism is well established physiology, but it should not be interpreted as proof that more glucose always means better hydration.</p><a href="/pages/glucose-water-absorption">Glucose & Water Absorption →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Sodium chloride</h3><p>Provides sodium and chloride, major extracellular ions that are also lost in sweat. Replacement needs vary substantially with diet, sweat rate, environment and individual health.</p><a href="/pages/electrolytes">Electrolytes →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Potassium chloride</h3><p>Provides potassium and chloride. Potassium is the major intracellular cation and contributes to normal cellular and neuromuscular physiology.</p><a href="/pages/five-essential-electrolytes">Five Electrolytes →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Sodium bicarbonate</h3><p>Provides sodium and bicarbonate. Bicarbonate is part of the body's extracellular acid-base buffer system. This ingredient role is distinct from high-dose sodium bicarbonate protocols studied in sport.</p><a href="/pages/why-bicarbonate">Why Bicarbonate →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Citric acid</h3><p>Contributes acidity and flavour balance, and participates in the product's citrate chemistry. Its presence does not turn the beverage into a clinical citrate therapy.</p><a href="/pages/ingredient-rationale">Ingredient Rationale →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Lemon powder</h3><p>Provides the lemonade flavour profile that makes the prepared drink easier to enjoy as part of a regular drinking routine.</p><a href="/pages/our-formula">Our Formula →</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

